# Generated artifacts whose `--check` gate compares content byte-for-byte.
#
# The generators write LF. With `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default) a
# fresh checkout rewrites text files as CRLF, and the byte comparison in
# `generate-examples.mjs`, `generate-package-graph.mjs` and
# `generate-toc-headings.mjs` would then report drift that does not exist — on
# every clone, for every contributor whose checkout converts line endings.
# Verified: with `core.autocrlf=true` a checkout of these blobs yields CRLF,
# which is not what the generators produce.
#
# Pinning them to LF keeps `--check` a real drift detector rather than a
# line-ending detector. These files are generated and never hand-edited, so
# the attribute costs nothing.
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow. `docs/reference/public-api.json` and the
# baselines under `docs/reference/baselines/` are read with `JSON.parse`, so
# line endings cannot affect them; they are left alone. Normalising the rest
# of the tree is a separate, repo-wide decision.
#
# If a byte-wise check is ever added for another generated path, pin it here.
apps/docs/lib/generated/** text eol=lf

# The API reference and CLI reference are generated and verified byte-for-byte
# by `generate-api-reference.mjs --check` and `generate-cli-reference.mjs
# --check`. On a CRLF checkout every one of the 61 + 14 files reports drift that
# is not drift. Pinned for the same reason as the manifest above.
apps/docs/content/api-reference/** text eol=lf
apps/docs/content/cli/** text eol=lf

# `readLibraries()` in `scripts/readme/canonical.mjs` splits `rawLibraries` on
# `\n  {\n`, so a CRLF checkout parses **zero** libraries and
# `pnpm docs:check-library-readmes` then passes while checking nothing. Pinned
# rather than made EOL-tolerant because the same source feeds
# `renderReadme()`, and CRs would leak into the generated READMEs and fail the
# byte comparison instead.
apps/docs/lib/libraries.tsx text eol=lf

# The documentation source itself.
#
# `apps/docs/scripts/generate-search-index.mjs` derives its entries from
# `content/`, `app/`, `components/` and `lib/`, and `test-search-index.mjs`
# regenerates and compares them. A CRLF working tree extracts fewer sections
# than an LF one, so an index generated on Windows and committed from there
# drifts on every LF checkout — which is what CI is. Measured before this pin:
# 2,148 entries from a CRLF tree against 2,267 from the same sources as LF, a
# 108-entry difference with nothing removed.
#
# This is the docs subtree the generators read, not a repo-wide normalisation:
# `packages/`, `examples/` and the root keep the default.
#
# Scoped to the text types the generators parse. The blanket `apps/docs/**`
# form marks the fonts and screenshots as text too, and a `text` attribute makes
# git normalise their bytes — `git diff` then reports every PNG as changed and a
# commit would store a mangled copy.
apps/docs/**/*.tsx text eol=lf
apps/docs/**/*.ts text eol=lf
apps/docs/**/*.md text eol=lf
apps/docs/**/*.mjs text eol=lf
apps/docs/**/*.json text eol=lf
apps/docs/**/*.css text eol=lf
apps/docs/**/*.txt text eol=lf
apps/docs/**/*.svg text eol=lf
apps/docs/**/*.png binary
apps/docs/**/*.woff2 binary

# The library-adapter READMEs are generated from the documentation recipes and
# verified byte-for-byte by `pnpm docs:check-library-readmes`. Same reasoning as
# above: with `core.autocrlf=true` a checkout would turn them into CRLF and the
# check would fail on every Windows clone for a reason that is not drift.
packages/adapters/*/README.md text eol=lf
